{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VFp8JZenx4T1PYSTQg7nLc","type":"tool_result","content":"site_name: ard\nsite_description: Automated repository development\nsite_url: https://albertcbrown.github.io/ard/\nrepo_url: https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard\ndocs_dir: docs/site\nstrict: true\ntheme:\n  name: material\n  features:\n    - navigation.sections\n    - search.suggest\nplugins:\n  - search\nnav:\n  - Overview: index.md\n  - Install: install.md\n  - Getting started: getting-started.md\n  - Configuration: configuration.md\n  - Roles: roles.md\n  - Operations:\n      - operations.md\n      - Gate 2 runbook: operations/gate-2-runbook.md\n  - Security model: security.md\n  - Troubleshooting: troubleshooting.md\n# Roles\n\nA role is a directory under `.ard/`. Its type is the parent directory, its name is the directory name, and `config.yaml` never lists it.\n\n| Directory | Type | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `.ard/scanners/<name>/` | scanner | Finds problems and files issues |\n| `.ard/triage/<name>/` | triage | Plans an issue |\n| `.ard/workers/<name>/` | worker | Implements a plan |\n| `.ard/reviewers/<name>/` | reviewer | Reviews a pull request |\n\nA role is an agent when it has a `prompt.txt`, and an executable (for scanners) when it has a `scan` script. A role with a `Dockerfile` runs in Docker; one without runs as a host subprocess.\n\n## Starter roles\n\n`ard init` ships these:\n\n- **Scanners:** `cve`, `docs`, `error-codes`, `lint`, `missing-tests`, `trufflehog`, `trufflehog-history`.\n- **Triage:** `lead` (routes the issue), `engineer` (writes the implementation plan), `product-manager`, `architect`, `designer`, `documentation-planner`.\n- **Workers:** `engineer`.\n- **Reviewers:** `eng-review`, `security-review`, `license-review`, `product-manager-review`.\n\nRole directories are protected paths in the recommended config, so changing one takes a human approval.\n\n## Writing a role prompt\n\n`prompt.txt` is the whole prompt. The controller gives the agent:\n\n- the issue and its comments as files, `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, which are untrusted text;\n- earlier roles' output under `.ard/<issue#>/`;\n- on a fix round, `test-output.log`;\n- an optional read-only sidecar repository at `$ARD_SIDECAR`.\n\nTell the agent to treat the input files as data and to write only its own output file. Say which status values it may report and what each means. Test a new prompt with `ard serve --dry-run` before relying on it.\n\n## The role file\n\nEach agent job ends by writing one file, `.ard/<issue#>/<role>.md` (workers write `worker-<role>.md`). It begins with frontmatter that code checks:\n\n- the first line is exactly `---`, and the frontmatter ends at the next line that is exactly `---`;\n- `role` is the role's name; `status` is `done`, `needs-human` or `blocked` (reviewers use their own verdicts); `attempt` is a number; `next` is an optional list of roles to run next.\n\nAnything else in the frontmatter fails the job. The body, after the closing `---`, becomes the comment the controller posts.\n\n## The write allowlist\n\nAn agent cannot push or edit GitHub. The controller commits a role's output only if every changed path is allowed:\n\n- every role may write its own role file;\n- a triage role may also write its own `## <section>` of `plan.md` (the heading named by its `plan_section`); the designer may also write under `.ard/<issue#>/design/`;\n- a worker may change code in its worktree, but never `.github/`, `.ard/` (other than its report), secret-shaped files, symlinks, binaries or files over 1 MiB, and never more changed lines than `diff_max_lines`.\n\nAny other change is rejected and the job ends `needs-human`. Nobody may change `issue.md`, another role's file or delete `plan.md`.\n# ard\n\nard makes a GitHub repository maintain itself. One `ard` process per repository runs a set of loops that share a small pool of job slots (`max_pods`, default 3). A deterministic controller owns every GitHub and git side effect. Agents only read and write files in a worktree.\n\n## The loop\n\n1. **Scanners** find problems (CVEs, lint, missing tests, docs gaps, secrets) and file deduplicated issues.\n2. **Triage** roles (lead, engineer, and optionally product manager, architect, designer, documentation planner) read an issue and write `plan.md`.\n3. **Workers** implement the plan on a `worker/...` branch, run the tests and open a draft pull request.\n4. **Reviewers** (engineering, security, license, product) critique the pull request and ask for fixes.\n5. **The merger** squash-merges a pull request once every review passed and its checks are green.\n6. **The Reaper** marks dead or overdue jobs `ard:executor:failed`.\n\n`.ard/<issue#>/` in your repository holds each issue's plan, per-role summaries and full logs.\n\n## The `ard:` labels\n\nGitHub labels prefixed `ard:` are the queue and the state machine. Most follow `ard:<stage>:<role>[:<state>]`, for example `ard:triage:lead:requested`. Run `ard setup` to create them. The ones you will touch:\n\n| Label | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `ard:pending` | Ready for triage. A maintainer adds it in `label` intake mode |\n| `ard:approved` | Human go-ahead for a worker to start |\n| `ard:needs-approval` | The pull request touches a protected path and waits for a human |\n| `ard:needs-human` | Blocked; automation stops until a human acts |\n| `ard:hold` | The controller ignores this issue or pull request |\n| `ard:executor:failed` | A job died or ran over time; remove the label to retry |\n| `ard:tests:failed` | The worker could not get the tests green |\n| `ard:keep` | Keep failed-job resources past 24 hours |\n| `ard:pause` | On the status issue: stop all new claims |\n\nThe full state machine is in the [design proposal](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/main/docs/proposal.md).\n\n## What humans still decide\n\n- **Approval.** With `require_approval` on (the default), a worker starts only after a human adds `ard:approved`.\n- **Protected paths.** A pull request touching any `merge.protected_paths` entry (for example `.github/`) waits for a human approval.\n- **Merges.** The merger lands pull requests that pass every gate. To keep merging by hand, start `ard serve` without the `merger` loop (see [Operations](operations.md)).\n- **Workflows.** Agents cannot edit `.github/workflows/`; a person does.\n\nNext: [Install](install.md), then [Getting started](getting-started.md). The design source of truth is the [proposal](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/main/docs/proposal.md).\ndocs/site/security.md:9:- **Write allowlists.** A role's output is committed only if every path is allowed (see [Roles](roles.md)).\nmkdocs.yml:19:  - Roles: roles.md\nmkdocs not found\n14:| `require_approval` | `true` | A worker starts only after a human adds `ard:approved` |\n94:| `max_review_rounds` | `3` | workers | Review rounds before `needs-human` |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"61c1f451-145c-4c06-8357-967729cffa65","uuid":"aa1d6a12-18af-4dc2-ba81-0153a5271cc0","timestamp":"2026-11-25T12:58:26.594Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"site_name: ard\nsite_description: Automated repository development\nsite_url: https://albertcbrown.github.io/ard/\nrepo_url: https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard\ndocs_dir: docs/site\nstrict: true\ntheme:\n  name: material\n  features:\n    - navigation.sections\n    - search.suggest\nplugins:\n  - search\nnav:\n  - Overview: index.md\n  - Install: install.md\n  - Getting started: getting-started.md\n  - Configuration: configuration.md\n  - Roles: roles.md\n  - Operations:\n      - operations.md\n      - Gate 2 runbook: operations/gate-2-runbook.md\n  - Security model: security.md\n  - Troubleshooting: troubleshooting.md\n# Roles\n\nA role is a directory under `.ard/`. Its type is the parent directory, its name is the directory name, and `config.yaml` never lists it.\n\n| Directory | Type | What it do