{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014aq8hp19nHHw2MWViLsVGp","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (35.1KB). Full output saved to: /ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M89CJ8SCZRPDDF8F5H1383WD/a8b61688-dee9-4362-9be1-21bb3426ffec/tool-results/bwyaz4rmz.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n## Scanners\n\nA scanner is a directory, `.ard/scanners/<name>/`, holding either an executable `scan` that prints findings as JSON lines or a `prompt.txt` for an LLM scanner. The controller does all dedupe, grouping, filing and closing, so deterministic tools and LLM scanners share one pipeline and no scanner holds a GitHub token.\n\n```\n{\"fingerprint\":\"osv:GHSA-xxxx:go.mod:golang.org/x/net\",\"title\":\"golang.org/x/net: GHSA-xxxx (high)\",\n \"severity\":\"high\",\"group\":\"go.mod\",\"files\":[\"go.mod\"],\"body\":\"markdown details\"}\n```\n\n**The output schema is strict.** Each line is one JSON object with exactly these keys; blank lines are skipped. `fingerprint` (required, 1 to 512 bytes of valid UTF-8, no control characters), `title` (required, one line, at most 200 characters), `severity` (required: `critical`, `high`, `medium`, `low` or `info`), `group` (optional; empty means one issue per finding), `files` (optional repo-relati\n\n**Scanner text is untrusted.** Titles and bodies are built from repository content (paths, identifiers, advisory text). The controller neutralises every title centrally, before filing or editing: control characters collapse to one space, backticks become apostrophes, `<`, `>` and link brackets are swapped for lookalike characters, a word-initial `@` becomes a fullwidth `＠` so nothing is notified, \n\n**The pre-filing guard cannot be turned off.** After every scan and before the diff, in dry-run too and even with zero findings, the controller scans the title, fingerprint, group, files and body of every finding with the builtin pattern scanner and the controller's known secret values, plus gitleaks when `redact.gitleaks.enabled`. A hit, a builtin-pass failure or an invalid line changes nothing: \n\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"61c1f451-145c-4c06-8357-967729cffa65","uuid":"aa555e09-f7f6-484a-8e37-075d4f1f6bbf","timestamp":"2026-11-25T12:58:29.166Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"## Scanners\n\nA scanner is a directory, `.ard/scanners/<name>/`, holding either an executable `scan` that prints findings as JSON lines or a `prompt.txt` for an LLM scanner. The controller does all dedupe, grouping, filing and closing, so deterministic tools and LLM scanners share one pipeline and no scanner holds a GitHub token.\n\n```\n{\"fingerprint\":\"osv:GHSA-xxxx:go.mod:golang.org/x/net\",\"title\":\"golang.org/x/net: GHSA-xxxx (high)\",\n \"severity\":\"high\",\"group\":\"go.mod\",\"files\":[\"go.mod\"],\"body\":\"markdown details\"}\n```\n\n**The output schema is strict.** Each line is one JSON object with exactly these keys; blank lines are skipped. `fingerprint` (required, 1 to 512 bytes of valid UTF-8, no control characters), `title` (required, one line, at most 200 characters), `severity` (required: `critical`, `high`, `medium`, `low` or `info`), `group` (optional; empty means one issue per finding), `files` (optional repo-relati\n\n**Scanner text is untrusted.** Titles and bodies are built from repository content (paths, identifiers, advisory text). The controller neutralises every title centrally, before filing or editing: control characters collapse to one space, backticks become apostrophes, `<`, `>` and link brackets are swapped for lookalike characters, a word-initial `@` becomes a fullwidth `＠` so nothing is notified, \n\n**The pre-filing guard cannot be turned off.** After every scan and before the diff, in dry-run too and even with zero findings, the controller scans the title, fingerprint, group, files and body of every finding with the builtin pattern scanner and the controller's known secret values, plus gitleaks when `redact.gitleaks.enabled`. A hit, a builtin-pass failure or an invalid line changes nothing: \n\n**LLM scanners (`prompt.txt`) file under extra rules.** `autoclose: true` on one is a config error: an LLM scanner never closes an issue for absence. `every` defaults to at least 24h and a value below 24h is an error; `max_budget_usd`, when set, must be above 0. An LLM fingerprint is `rule:path` or `rule:path:symbol` (rule `[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,63}`, a clean relative path without `..` that is also \n\n**Route.** The scanner key `route` decides where its issues go: `pending` (default for executable scanners) files with `ard:pending`, so they enter triage; `none` (default for LLM scanners) files with the scanner label only, and a human adds `ard:pending` to send one issue to triage; `needs-human` files with `ard:needs-human` and no `ard:pending`, so no agent triages them. LLM scanners may not use\n\n**Each run, in order:**\n\n1. **Reconcile.** Fetch the scanner's open issues, plus a lookup of each issue that has a tracked finding and is no longer open, and update its state. Every marker line on an open issue becomes a row on that issue (a fingerprint on two open issues goes to the lowest number), so a crash between creating an issue and recording it heals here instead of filing a duplicate. Closed as completed: mark th\n2. **Backpressure, in code, before the scanner starts.** The controller counts open issues with `ard:scanner:<name>`; at or above `max_open` it skips the run and logs it, autoclose scanners included (`none` and `needs-human` issues count too, and are never stale-closed, so a non-autoclose scanner with those routes stops once its issues fill `max_open` until a human closes or promotes some): a scan\n3. **Scan.** Run the scanner on a read-only worktree of `origin/main`, with a timeout. It needs a free job slot, and scanners rank last for one. **A run that is denied a slot is not dropped: it is not recorded, stays due and is offered again on the next tick** (every five minutes), until it gets a slot or the controller stops. A failed scan (non-zero exit, timeout, invalid output) is recorded and \n4. **Diff.** A new, unsuppressed fingerprint is queued for filing. A suppressed fingerprint stays quiet unless its severity rises above the suppressed one. A fingerprint that returns after being fixed files a new issue that links the old one as a regression. An open issue whose fingerprints are all gone gets \"No longer detected at `<sha>`\" and is closed, but only for scanners marked `autoclose` (d\n5. **Group.** Findings that share a `group` key join one issue, up to `group_size` (N), as a checklist with one line per finding (ticked lines count towards N). An empty `group` means one issue per finding. New findings join an open group issue of the same group that has room and is not yet claimed, meaning it carries no `ard:` label besides `ard:scanner:<name>` and the route's label (none under `\n6. **File.** Edits and closes first, then joins, then new issues. Create the issue with `ard:scanner:<name>` plus the route's label (`ard:pending` by default), then queue `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` to the committer. The issue comes first because its number names the directory. A committer failure is logged and does not undo the issue: its body already carries the checklist. **An issue with `ard:hold\n\n**A scanner loop runs at most one due scanner per tick.** The `scanners` loop polls every five minutes, picks the most overdue scanner (last run plus its `every`; a dropped run counts as a run) and runs it. Pausing stops it, because it files and closes issues. Its tick timeout is the longest scan timeout plus 15 minutes, not the poll interval.\n\n**Each scanner has its own state, and GitHub is the truth.** Every scanner keeps a separate SQLite file, `~/.ard/state/<owner>/<repo>/<scanner>.db`: fingerprint to issue, first and last seen, suppressions, last run. Scanners never share or lock each other's state, and one can be reset without touching the rest. The controller keeps its own jobs, attempts, events and a small key/value table (for ex\n\n`ard rebuild-state <scanner>` takes the leader lock (it refuses to run while `ard serve` holds it), reads every issue with `ard:scanner:<name>`, open and closed, and replaces the scanner's findings and suppressions in one transaction, keeping the run history. For each fingerprint, an open issue wins (the lowest number), else the highest-numbered closed issue decides: completed leaves a `fixed` tom\n\n**Dry-run files nothing.** Under `--dry-run` a would-be issue gets a synthetic negative number, stored in the scanner's dry-run database like a real one, so repeated scans do not re-propose it and `max_open` is reached from synthetic issues. The committer is never called for a synthetic number. The global cap counts only the running scanner's synthetic issues, since scanners share no state.\n\n**Closed is not always \"forget\".** Removing a fingerprint on every close, as first sketched, refiles a won't-fix issue on the next run. Completed closes stop tracking the finding but keep a `fixed` tombstone, so a finding that comes back is filed as a regression of the old issue. Not-planned closes, and closes as a duplicate, suppress, and re-raise early if severity rises.\n\n**LLM scanners need stable fingerprints.** Their prose changes run to run, so they fingerprint on rule, file and symbol, never on wording. Before filing, the controller also compares the title against the scanner's open issues and skips near-duplicates.\n\n**The stale closer is a built-in scanner.** It has no role directory and needs no job slot, executor or worktree; its settings live in the top-level `stale:` block of `config.yaml`. It is a plain controller loop that runs every 24 hours and is skipped while paused. It closes scanner issues still at `ard:pending` with no activity (issues filed under `route: none` or `needs-human` lack `ard:pending`\n\n| Scanner | Engine | Every | Group size | Auto-close |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| cve | osv-scanner and govulncheck (trivy and the Dependabot alerts API later) | 6 h | 1 | yes |\n| lts | endoflife.date v1 API + manifests: go.mod, package.json, Dockerfile `FROM`, Helm `values.yaml` images | 7 d | 1 per component (product and cycle) | yes |\n| lint | golangci-lint per Go module (the repo's config, or the role's `golangci.yml`), plus `//nolint` directives, minus the role directory's `baseline` | 24 h | 20 per rule and directory | yes |\n| error-codes | pattern match over source, registry and docs files (`sh` + `jq`) | 24 h | 20 | yes |\n| trufflehog | leaked-credential scan of the current commit (`sh` + `jq` + TruffleHog) | 24 h | 1 | yes |\n| missing-tests | `go test` coverage plus exported functions with no test (`sh` + Go helper) | 24 h | 10 per package | yes |\n| trufflehog-history | leaked-credential scan of the full git history (same tools) | 7 d | 1 | no |\n| docs | undocumented exported Go identifiers and package comments (deterministic) | 3 d | 10 per package | yes |\n| docs-drift | LLM: docs that contradict the code (planned; needs the LLM scanner engine, #204) | 3 d | 10 per doc file | no |\n| architecture | LLM against ADRs and the sidecar spec repo | 3 d | 1 | no |\n| stale | built-in | 24 h | none | none |\n\n**The cve scanner** (`scanners/cve`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program, no build step) merges osv-scanner (all ecosystems) and govulncheck (Go reachability). Its fingerprint is `osv:<advisory>:<manifest>:<package>`, where the advisory is the smallest `GHSA-` id of the vulnerability, else the smallest `CVE-` id, else the smallest id, so one vulnerability reported under `GO-`, `GHSA-` and `CVE-` i\n\n**The lint scanner** (`scanners/lint`, a `sh` script, one `jq` program and a Dockerfile that builds golangci-lint v2 with the image's Go) is Go only in v1; other ecosystems are follow-ups. It runs golangci-lint once per `go.mod` (pruning `.git`, `vendor`, `node_modules` and `testdata`) with the repo's own config, or a human-owned `golangci.yml` in the role directory when present. These flags are a\n\n**The error-codes scanner** (`scanners/error-codes`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program, pattern match over source, registry and docs files) reads a human-owned `codes.json` in its role directory; nothing ships one, because error-code conventions are specific to each repository. Keys: `pattern` (required, a regex of at most 512 characters, wrapped so `E1001` does not match inside `XE10011`; it mu\n\n**The missing-tests scanner** (`scanners/missing-tests`, a `sh` script plus a stdlib-only Go helper) runs each Go module's own tests with `go test -coverpkg=./...` and files one finding per exported function or method that no test calls by name or that the tests never run; the group is the package, and the fingerprint does not depend on the file or line. Because it runs the repository's tests it n\n\n**The lts scanner** (`scanners/lts`, a `sh` script plus two `jq` programs and `yq` for Helm, no build step) flags components at or near end of life using the endoflife.date v1 API (`/api/v1/products/<slug>`, one request per referenced product). A component is a product plus a release cycle. Its fingerprint is `eol:<product>:<cycle>` (for example `eol:nodejs:18`), one finding lists every referencin\n\n**The trufflehog scanner** (`scanners/trufflehog`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program) runs `trufflehog filesystem . --json --no-update --no-color --no-verification --no-ignore-tag --fail-on-scan-errors --concurrency=2` on the current commit. It never verifies a credential against a provider. Only the detector name, file, line and a hash of the secret leave the scan: `Raw`, `RawV2`, `Redacted`, `\n\n**The trufflehog-history scanner** (`scanners/trufflehog-history`) runs `trufflehog git file://$ARD_HISTORY --bare` with the same flags plus no `--since-commit`, `--max-depth` or `--branch`, over every fetched ref, with the same hash salt, so a value has the same fingerprint as at HEAD. A scanner role asks for history by holding an empty file named `history` in its directory (`Role.ReadsHistory`; \n\n**The docs scanner** (`scanners/docs`, a `sh` script plus one stdlib-only Go program, `docscan.go`, built at scan time with the job's own Go) lists undocumented exported Go identifiers. It reports, per package directory: a missing package comment; an exported function, a method on an exported receiver type (pointer, value or generic) and an exported type with no doc comment (the spec's doc, its tr\n\nIntervals and group sizes are starting values to tune, not measurements.\n\n## Triage loop\n\nTriage turns an issue into a `plan.md` a worker can execute, one role at a time, and writes only to `.ard/<issue#>/` on `main`.\n\n**Each poll (every 15 minutes by default), for each triage role:**\n\n1. Query for the role's request label (the lead's is `ard:pending`), excluding claimed, done, failed, blocked and held issues. Which human-filed issues receive `ard:pending` is the `intake` setting (decided, #2): in `all` mode every human-filed issue enters with no label needed; in `label` mode only issues carrying the configured intake label do. `intake.Admit` decides, as a pure function of the i\n2. Wait for a free job slot (triage ranks below reviewers and workers), then claim the oldest match: add `ard:triage:<role>:running` and record the job (id, executor, start, deadline). Claiming runs in a watcher goroutine; at most one triage job per issue; under `--dry-run` the loop logs `triage.would_claim` and creates no job, slot or label.\n3. Prepare a detached worktree at `origin/main`, write the untrusted snapshot to `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (never committed; the committed `issue.md` stays owned by the scanner), and mount the role's sidecar repo read-only at `$ARD_SIDECAR`.\n4. Run the agent CLI with `.ard/triage/<role>/prompt.txt` and the issue directory as context.\n5. Close out: read `triage-<role>.md` frontmatter, send the issue directory (and nothing else) to the committer, post the summary as a comment, swap `:running` for `:done`, and apply any requests.\n\n**The lead routes; the controller applies.** The lead writes `next: [triage:architect, triage:product-manager]` and `worker: engineer` in `triage-lead.md`. The controller adds the matching `ard:` labels only if each names a role directory that exists.\n\n**Starter planning roles.** `ard init` also ships four planning roles, each writing its own `plan.md` section (`config.BuiltinPlanSections`) and its role file: `product-manager` (`## Requirements`: requirements, a numbered list of acceptance criteria, out of scope), `architect` (`## Design`: approach, affected modules, and an `ADR needed: yes|no` line; the worker writes any ADR in its pull request\n\nThe starter lead (`.ard/triage/lead/prompt.txt`) follows a fixed contract. It reads the issue only through the untrusted input files `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, which the triage loop writes and never commits. It finds roles by listing the role directories and routes only to those. It writes only `triage-lead.md`, with `attempt: 1`, and a `status: done` always names a `worker`\n\nThe starter engineer (`.ard/triage/engineer/prompt.txt`) plans for the worker without implementing. Its inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, all untrusted. It writes the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md` with Steps, Test plan, Risk and Size, sized S, M, L or XL on a scale tied to the worker diff guard's block limit (XL is over the worker role\n\n**A deterministic gate starts the worker.** Once per triage tick, after the intake sweep, the controller searches for open issues that carry `ard:triage:lead:done` and none of `ard:pending`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold`, `ard:executor:failed` or any configured worker's request label (the exclusion keeps gated issues from filling the 100-hit window). It re-reads each hit and decides from labels al\n\nThree cases go to a human, each with one comment and `ard:needs-human`, and nothing is ever closed automatically: the lead finished `done` but named no valid worker (or its close-out record is missing), the chosen worker role no longer exists, or an `ard:triage:<role>` is requested for a role that does not exist. A title or body edit after admission, on a human-filed issue, also stops the gate wit\n\nBefore it asks for approval or starts a worker, the gate checks that `.ard/<n>/plan.md` exists on `main`. If it does not, the gate posts one comment, adds `ard:needs-human` and records a `gate` event with reason `no-plan`; a check that fails to run is retried on the next tick. Removing a role's request label by hand does not count as that role finishing. With `require_approval: true` (the Phase 1 \n\n**Order comes from config, not the lead.** A role can declare `after: [architect, product-manager]`; the controller holds its request until those roles, if requested, are done. A prerequisite is requested when its request label (`ard:pending` for the lead) or its `:running` label is present, and done when its `:done` label is present; one that was never requested does not hold. A held role is logg\n\n| Role | Adds to plan.md | Typical trigger |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lead | nothing (role file only); writes `triage-lead.md` with routing, or `needs-human` when a person must decide | every `ard:pending` issue |\n| product-manager | `## Requirements`: user value, acceptance criteria, out of scope | feature asks |\n| architect | `## Design`: approach, affected modules, ADR needed or not | cross-module change, new dependency |\n| designer | `## UX`, plus design files in `.ard/<issue#>/design/` | UI change |\n| documentation-planner | `## Docs`: which pages change and what they must say | public API or behaviour change |\n| engineer | `## Implementation`: steps, test plan, risk, size | every issue that reaches a worker |\n\nEach triage role owns one `##` section of `plan.md` with a fixed heading (Requirements, Design, UX, Docs, Implementation; a custom triage role names its own with `plan_section`). The first writer creates the file with `# Plan for #<issue#>`. A role replaces its own section in place on a re-run, never edits another role's section (close-out enforces it), and leaves the file untouched on `blocked` o\n\n**Sidecar repos are context, read-only.** A role's sidecar (for example `vpe-specs`) is cloned by the executor, not the loop: the loop passes the role's resolved sidecar (repo and ref) on the job. The executor keeps one shallow (depth 1) bare object cache per sidecar repo at `~/.ard/sidecars/<controlled-owner>/<controlled-repo>/<sidecar-owner>/<sidecar-repo>.git`, shared by every job of that contr\n=====\n## Worker loop\n\nA worker implements `plan.md` on its own branch and ends with a draft PR, test results, and a summary on the issue.\n\n1. **Poll and claim** `ard:worker:<role>` (added by the triage fan-in gate; the worker never adds or removes it) like triage, also skipping `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold`, `ard:executor:failed` and an issue that has a running triage job or any running worker job. Workers rank second for job slots, after reviewers. Before claiming, the loop re-checks `ard:approved` when `require_approval` is on (no \n2. **Branch** `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>` from `origin/main`. The slug is the issue title lowercased, every run of other characters turned into one `-`, at most 40 ASCII characters, `issue` when nothing is left. The title can change between rounds, so the loop looks the branch up with `git ls-remote` on `worker/<role>-<issue#>-*`: one match is reused (and fetched), none is created on the first\n3. **Steps.** One worker job is one state row, one pool slot and one attempt, but it runs several executor jobs, each in a fresh worktree detached at a SHA: the agent step (`<job id>`) at the branch head, or `origin/main` for a new branch; a test step (`<job id>t<k>`) and a fix step (`<job id>f<k>`) at the SHA the last push made. The branch itself belongs to the committer. Each step's handle is sa\n4. **Run the agent** with `.ard/workers/<role>/prompt.txt` and these untrusted inputs: `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (written by the loop, outside `.ard/<issue#>/`, never committed), `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md` when triage wrote one and `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` when a scanner filed the issue (each only as a regular file, never through a symlink), the triage role files and logs, and,\n5. **Guard the diff** after each agent or fix step, before anything leaves the host. The worktree belongs to the agent, so the guard never trusts its `.git`, config, index or attributes: git runs against the controller's own clone with a private index filled from the base commit (so `skip-worktree` and `assume-unchanged` hide nothing, and no planted filter, textconv, fsmonitor or hook runs), with \n6. **Two commits per round.** The loop commits the code through the committer's code lane (`Branches.CommitCode`) with the title `[<type>: ]<issue title> (#<issue>)`, the redacted summary (secrets and host paths) as the body and an `Ard-Job: <job-id>` trailer, no `[skip ci]`. `<type>` comes from the issue's `type:*` label (`feature` is `feat`, `bug` is `fix`; `docs`, `chore`, `refactor` and `test`\n7. **Test.** Run `tests` (default `make test`; empty disables the step) as `/bin/sh -c` in the role's image, with no credential. On failure the agent gets the failure output in a fix step, which commits and pushes again, up to `max_test_fixes` (default 2) within the same job. A fix that changes no files, the cap, or the time running out ends the loop with the tests failed. Test logs stay on the ho\n8. **Open or update a draft PR** with ard's own `Closes #<issue>`, the agent summary quoted and neutralised (`committer.NeutraliseRefs`, then marker and mention defusing), the test result and links to `worker-<role>.md`, the logs and the job id. Reviewers are chosen by `config.SelectReviewers`: the candidates are the role's `reviewers` (a list that only narrows), or every reviewer role when it is \n9. **Report.** Comment on the issue with the summary, PR link and test result (`passed`, `passed after 1 fix attempt`, `failed after 2 fix attempts`, `not configured`); swap `ard:worker:<role>:running` for `:done`. If tests still fail, add `ard:tests:failed` on the issue and the PR and request no reviewers; a green run removes it. A worker that ends without the loop's result (for example one the R\n\n**The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role files and logs, in a review round `review-feedback.md` and the reviewer role files and logs. It wri\n\n**Review feedback loops back here.** The `reviewfix` loop (#41) starts a round when every requested reviewer has finished and at least one ended `ard:review:<role>:changes`, backed by that role's close-out record (a label alone does not count). It records a `review_round` event in `_controller.db` (counted when requested, ordered by event ID, local to that database) and removes `ard:worker:<role>:\n\n**The merger takes it from here.** A worker PR stays a draft until every requested review passes at its current head; then the controller marks it ready for review. The merger merges ready PRs that also pass its gates.\n\n## Reviewer loop\n\nReviewers run like workers against a PR instead of an issue: they read the change, never edit code, and return a verdict the controller turns into PR comments and a label.\n\n1. **Poll and claim** open PRs with `ard:review:<role>`, skipping claimed, done, changes, failed and held. Reviewers get first call on free job slots, since each finished review can unblock a merge. Only PRs the App opened are reviewed: the author is the App, the base is `main` and the head is a `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>` branch (human PRs are skipped and logged as `not_app_pr`). A PR is also\n2. **Pin the head SHA.** Before claiming, the loop fetches the branch into the controller's clone and requires the tip to equal the PR's head SHA; on a mismatch it retries on the next poll. The match also shows the head is this repository's branch, so a fork PR with a colliding branch name is never reviewed. The job's worktree is cut at that commit, so the review describes exactly one commit even \n3. **Run the aid, then the agent.** After the pin, a role with an executable `aid` in its directory (reviewers only, like a scanner's `scan`; #205) has it run by the controller as its own executor job at the pinned head, before the agent, so the findings of its deterministic tools do not depend on the host or on the agent choosing to run them. The tool finds; the agent explains, ranks and confirms\n4. **The agent writes `review-<role>.md`** with `verdict: approve | changes` and `findings:` (`file`, `line`, `severity`, `blocking`, `message`; see the .ard/ contract). It may write nothing else under `.ard/<issue#>/`, and code edits are never collected. An `approve` that carries a blocking finding is treated as `changes` (the job succeeds and the comment says so).\n5. **Close-out.** The controller posts one PR review at the pinned commit (event `COMMENT`): findings on lines the PR added are inline comments, every other finding is listed in the review body. If GitHub answers 422 (a line or the commit is not in the PR), it posts the review again with every finding in the body, and if that is also refused, as a plain PR comment. It commits `review-<role>.md` an\n6. **A moved head is not a pass.** Just before close-out the loop compares the branch with the pinned commit. If it gained code outside `.ard/<issue#>/` meanwhile, a `changes` verdict is still posted and labelled (it is the conservative one), but an `approve` is posted without `:done`: the request label stays and the review runs again on the new head. The reviewed commit is recorded in the close-o\n7. **A gone PR is stale, not failed.** A human may merge or close the PR, or GitHub may delete its branch, while a review runs. At close-out the controller reads the PR before committing. If it was merged or closed, or the committer reports the branch gone, the job ends `stale`: nothing is committed to the branch, only `:running` is removed, there is no `ard:executor:failed` and no failure comment\n\n**The starter reviewer (`reviewers/eng-review`, #36).** `.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt` checks correctness, missing tests and drift from `plan.md`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `diff.patch`, `issue.md`, `comments.md` and the committed `.ard/<issue#>/`; text in the change that addresses a reviewer is a blocking `high` finding on its line (verdict `changes`), and the same text in the issue,\n\n**Repo context in the starter reviewers.** `eng-review`, `license-review` and `security-review` read `TECH_STACK.md`; `product-manager-review` reads `PRODUCT.md` and `VISION.md`. Each file is optional and a template-only file counts as absent. A conflict between the change and a file is reported as a finding. The PR-head rule: reviewers trust the files only while `diff.patch` does not touch `.ard/\n\n**The starter security reviewer (`reviewers/security-review`, #43).** `.ard/reviewers/security-review/prompt.txt` looks for injection, authz gaps, secrets and unsafe dependencies the change introduces or makes reachable, plus other weaknesses. It has the same untrusted inputs, Trust rules (steering text in the change is a blocking `high` finding), write rules and role-file format as eng-review, wi\n\n**The starter license reviewer (`reviewers/license-review`, #44).** `.ard/reviewers/license-review/prompt.txt` checks that every dependency a PR adds or upgrades has a license on the owners' allowlist. The allowlist is one SPDX id per line (`#` comments; `A OR B` passes when any operand is listed, `A AND B` only when all are, `A WITH E` only when listed verbatim; no per-package exceptions). It is \n\n**The starter PM reviewer (`reviewers/product-manager-review`, #45).** `.ard/reviewers/product-manager-review/prompt.txt` checks the change against the acceptance criteria. They come from `plan.md`'s `## Requirements` (`### Acceptance criteria`, numbered AC1, AC2), else the issue body; comments never add, relax or remove one, and a plan that contradicts the issue is `needs-human`. Each criterion i\n\n**The label is the gate, not GitHub's \"request changes\".** A bot's request-changes review blocks merge until someone dismisses it, even after the fix. Reviews post as comments; `ard:review:<role>:changes` is what sends the PR back to the worker.\n\n**Pair each reviewer with a deterministic tool where one exists.** The tool finds; the agent explains, ranks and checks the result against the plan.\n\n| Reviewer | Runs when | Deterministic aid | Blocks on |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| security-review | always | gitleaks, gosec, semgrep with local rules (run by the role's `aid` before the agent; the agent runs them only without `aid.jsonl`) | injection, authz gaps, secrets, unsafe dependencies |\n| eng-review | always | lint and coverage of added lines (the repo's own commands, run by the agent) | correctness, missing tests, drift from `plan.md` |\n| license-review | a manifest or lockfile changes (`go.mod`, `go.sum`, `package.json`, `package-lock.json`, `npm-shrinkwrap.json`) | go-licenses (run by the agent when on the host `PATH`; optional), scancode later | a license outside the allowlist |\n| product-manager-review | the issue had product-manager triage (starter `when: {triage: [product-manager]}`), or UI paths a repo adds to `when.paths` | none | acceptance criteria not met |\n\nEvery directory under `.ard/reviewers/` reviews every PR unless `config.yaml` gives it a `when:` rule (paths, or a triage role that ran; either is enough, and a `when:` with neither is an error). A worker's `reviewers:` list narrows the candidates but does not bypass `when:`. The lead (or the worker) can request extra reviewers through `next`, which bypasses `when:`; they are recorded in the triag\n\n## Merger loop\n\nThe merger is controller code, not an agent, and takes no job slot. Every 5 minutes it merges at most one PR that has cleared every gate, oldest first, and the merge closes the issue. It also updates at most one behind branch per tick, so CI capacity goes t","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M89CJ8SCZRPDDF8F5H1383WD/a8b61688-dee9-4362-9be1-21bb3426ffec/tool-results/bwyaz4rmz.txt","persistedOutputSize":35899},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_014aq8hp19nHHw2MWViLsVGp","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
