{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CeYtGEkgdkQ6HnD4cSmHp1","type":"tool_result","content":"## Label state machine\n\nEvery label starts with `ard:`, and every stage uses one grammar: `ard:<stage>:<role>` requests work, `:running` marks a claim, `:done` marks completion. A loop picks up an issue that has the request label and none of `:running`, `:done`, `ard:executor:failed`\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> Requested: prior stage sets ard:STAGE:ROLE\n    Requested --> Running: claim (+ :running)\n    Running --> Done: close-out (+ :done)\n    Running --> Failed: dead or overdue (ard:executor:failed)\n    Running --> Changes: review verdict (+ :changes)\n    Failed --> Requested: human retries\n    Changes --> [*]: worker re-requested\n    Done --> [*]: next stage requested\n    note right of Running\n        ard:hold, ard:needs-human and ard:pause\n        stop the machine at any state\n    end note\n```\n\n`:done` hands off to the next stage's request label, `:changes` sends the PR back to the worker, and a failed job waits until a human removes `ard:executor:failed`.\n\n| Label | Set by | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `ard:scanner:<name>` | Scanner | Which scanner owns the issue; used for dedupe and backpressure |\n| `ard:pending` | Scanner or human | Ready for triage; the lead's request label |\n| `ard:triage:<role>` | Lead (via controller) or human | This triage role is requested |\n| `ard:worker:<role>` | Triage gate (controller) | Plan is complete; ready to implement |\n| `ard:review:<role>` | Worker close-out (on the PR) | This review is requested |\n| `ard:<stage>:<role>:running` | Controller on claim | An executor holds the job |\n| `ard:<stage>:<role>:done` | Controller on close-out | Finished; output is in `.ard/<issue#>/` |\n| `ard:review:<role>:changes` | Controller on close-out | Reviewer found blocking issues; sends the PR back to the worker |\n| `ard:executor:failed` | Reaper or close-out | Job died or ran over time; resources kept 24 h |\n| `ard:needs-human` | Controller, from the agent's result, a scanner at filing (`route: needs-human`), the merger, or the PR watch loop (a PR closed without merging while its issue is open) | Agent reported it is blocked, or the PR is gone; automation stops |\n| `ard:needs-approval` | Merger | PR touches a protected path; merges after a write-access human approves its head with a GitHub review |\n| `ard:hold` | Human | Controller ignores this issue or PR entirely |\n\nFour more labels sit outside the grammar: `ard:approved` (human go-ahead before a worker starts; counts only when a triage-or-higher collaborator added it after triage finished), `ard:tests:failed` (worker could not get tests green; set by worker close-out on \n\n**One query per role per poll.** For example: `is:open label:\"ard:triage:architect\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:running\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:done\" -label:ard:executor:failed -label:ard:needs-human -label:ard:hold`. Six triage roles every 15 minutes, \n\n**Claims need a single owner, not atomic labels.** GitHub labels are not a lock: two pollers can both add `:running`. Phase 1 runs one controller per repo behind a host lock (`flock` locally, `flock` in both modes; in-cluster the lock file is on the `-home` PV\n\n**Agents never touch labels.** The executor's close-out removes `:running`, adds `:done` (or `ard:executor:failed`), and posts the summary comment. `ard:pending` is removed when the lead finishes, so it means \"not yet triaged\" and nothing else.\n\n**Retries are explicit.** Every retry, automatic or human, is a new job with its own id (and attempt 1). A human retries a failed job by removing `ard:executor:failed`. The controller retries on its own once, only for a transient cause it can name: a rate limi","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"61c1f451-145c-4c06-8357-967729cffa65","uuid":"0e69c605-2a76-4a21-9cee-ff937ef213f2","timestamp":"2026-11-25T12:58:32.024Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"## Label state machine\n\nEvery label starts with `ard:`, and every stage uses one grammar: `ard:<stage>:<role>` requests work, `:running` marks a claim, `:done` marks completion. A loop picks up an issue that has the request label and none of `:running`, `:done`, `ard:executor:failed`\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> Requested: prior stage sets ard:STAGE:ROLE\n    Requested --> Running: claim (+ :running)\n    Running --> Done: close-out (+ :done)\n    Running --> Failed: dead or overdue (ard:executor:failed)\n    Running --> Changes: review verdict (+ :changes)\n    Failed --> Requested: human retries\n    Changes --> [*]: worker re-requested\n    Done --> [*]: next stage requested\n    note right of Running\n        ard:hold, ard:needs-human and ard:pause\n        stop the machine at any state\n    end note\n```\n\n`:done` hands off to the next stage's request label, `:changes` sends the PR back to the worker, and a failed job waits until a human removes `ard:executor:failed`.\n\n| Label | Set by | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `ard:scanner:<name>` | Scanner | Which scanner owns the issue; used for dedupe and backpressure |\n| `ard:pending` | Scanner or human | Ready for triage; the lead's request label |\n| `ard:triage:<role>` | Lead (via controller) or human | This triage role is requested |\n| `ard:worker:<role>` | Triage gate (controller) | Plan is complete; ready to implement |\n| `ard:review:<role>` | Worker close-out (on the PR) | This review is requested |\n| `ard:<stage>:<role>:running` | Controller on claim | An executor holds the job |\n| `ard:<stage>:<role>:done` | Controller on close-out | Finished; output is in `.ard/<issue#>/` |\n| `ard:review:<role>:changes` | Controller on close-out | Reviewer found blocking issues; sends the PR back to the worker |\n| `ard:executor:failed` | Reaper or close-out | Job died or ran over time; resources kept 24 h |\n| `ard:needs-human` | Controller, from the agent's result, a scanner at filing (`route: needs-human`), the merger, or the PR watch loop (a PR closed without merging while its issue is open) | Agent reported it is blocked, or the PR is gone; automation stops |\n| `ard:needs-approval` | Merger | PR touches a protected path; merges after a write-access human approves its head with a GitHub review |\n| `ard:hold` | Human | Controller ignores this issue or PR entirely |\n\nFour more labels sit outside the grammar: `ard:approved` (human go-ahead before a worker starts; counts only when a triage-or-higher collaborator added it after triage finished), `ard:tests:failed` (worker could not get tests green; set by worker close-out on \n\n**One query per role per poll.** For example: `is:open label:\"ard:triage:architect\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:running\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:done\" -label:ard:executor:failed -label:ard:needs-human -label:ard:hold`. Six triage roles every 15 minutes, \n\n**Claims need a single owner, not atomic labels.** GitHub labels are not a lock: two pollers can both add `:running`. Phase 1 runs one controller per repo behind a host lock (`flock` locally, `flock` in both modes; in-cluster the lock file is on the `-home` PV\n\n**Agents never touch labels.** The executor's close-out removes `:running`, adds `:done` (or `ard:executor:failed`), and posts the summary comment. `ard:pending` is removed when the lead finishes, so it means \"not yet triaged\" and nothing else.\n\n**Retries are explicit.** Every retry, automatic or human, is a new job with its own id (and attempt 1). A human retries a failed job by removing `ard:executor:failed`. The controller retries on its own once, only for a transient cause it can name: a rate limi","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01CeYtGEkgdkQ6HnD4cSmHp1","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChPgjrNjHZ8i6EqKWhCcc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now write the files."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1513,"cache_read_input_tokens":23585,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1513},"output_tokens":27,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"61c1f451-145c-4c06-8357-967729cffa65","uuid":"9971e728-631f-4586-853f-1a8c37cf9f70","timestamp":"2026-11-25T12:58:32.872Z","request_id":"req_011ChPgjrDZASsRz11S6187Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChPgjrNjHZ8i6EqKWhCcc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XWfUb2WuNQMmaib8WrFpAz","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"mkdir -p docs/site/roles && cd docs/site && python3 - <<'EOF'\nold=open('roles.md').read()\ni=old.index('## Writing a role prompt')\ntail=old[i:]\nhead='''# Roles overview\n\nA role is a directory under `.ard/`. Its type is the parent directory, its name is the directory name, and `config.yaml` never lists it.\n\n| Directory | Type | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `.ard/scanners/<name>/` | scanner | Finds problems and files issues |\n| `.ard/triage/<name>/` | triage | Plans an issue |\n| `.ard/workers/<name>/` | worker | Implements a plan |\n| `.ard/reviewers/<name>/` | reviewer | Reviews a pull request |\n\nA role is an agent when it has a `prompt.txt`, and an executable (for scanners) when it has a `scan` script. A role with a `Dockerfile` runs in Docker; one without runs as a host subprocess.\n\n## The four kinds\n\n- [Scanners](scanners.md) find problems and file issues.\n- [Triage](triage.md) roles plan an issue.\n- [Workers](workers.md) implement a plan.\n- [Reviewers](reviewers.md) review the pull request.\n\nEach page lists the starter roles `ard init` ships for that kind.\n\nRole directories are protected paths in the recommended config, so changing one takes a human approval.\n\n'''\nopen('roles/index.md','w').write(head+tail)\nEOF\ncat > roles/scanners.md <<'EOF'\n# Scanners\n\n## What it does\n\nA scanner finds problems (CVEs, lint, missing tests, docs gaps, secrets) and files issues. It is a directory, `.ard/scanners/<name>/`, holding either an executable `scan` that prints findings as JSON lines or a `prompt.txt` for an LLM scanner. The controller does all dedupe, grouping, filing and closing, so both kinds share one pipeline and no scanner holds a GitHub token.\n\n## Inputs and outputs\n\n- **Output:** one JSON object per line with a fingerprint, a title, a severity and optionally a group, files and a body. The schema is strict; a line that does not fit is an invalid line.\n- Scanner text is untrusted. The controller neutralises titles and bodies before filing, and scans every finding for secrets before anything is filed. That check cannot be turned off.\n- A finding with the same fingerprint as an open issue is not filed twice.\n\n## Labels\n\n- `ard:scanner:<name>` marks the issue's owning scanner, used for dedupe and backpressure.\n- `ard:pending` sends a filed issue to triage. The `route` override can instead file with the scanner label only, or with `ard:needs-human`.\n\n## Starter roles\n\n`cve`, `docs`, `error-codes`, `lint`, `missing-tests`, `trufflehog`, `trufflehog-history`.\n\n## Config overrides\n\nSet these under `overrides` for `scanners/<name>` (see [Configuration](../configuration.md)): `every`, `max_open`, `group_size`, `autoclose`, `route`, `timeout`. The global `intervals.scanners` and `global_max_open` apply to all scanners.\nEOF\ncat > roles/triage.md <<'EOF'\n# Triage\n\n## What it does\n\nTriage roles read an issue and write the plan for it. The `lead` routes the issue to the other triage roles it needs, and the `engineer` writes the implementation plan. Their sections together make up `plan.md`. When every requested triage role is done, and a human has added `ard:approved` (unless `require_approval` is off), the issue goes to a worker.\n\n## Inputs and outputs\n\n- **Inputs:** the issue and its comments (untrusted text), and earlier roles' output under `.ard/<issue#>/`.\n- **Outputs:** the role's own file, `.ard/<issue#>/<role>.md`, and its own `## <section>` of `plan.md`. The designer may also write under `.ard/<issue#>/design/`.\n\n## Labels\n\n- `ard:pending` is the lead's request label. It is removed when the lead finishes.\n- `ard:triage:<role>` requests a triage role; `:running` and `:done` follow the claim and the close-out.\n- `ard:worker:<role>` is set once the plan is complete.\n- `ard:approved` is the human go-ahead before a worker starts.\n- `ard:needs-human` stops automation when a role reports it is blocked.\n\n## Starter roles\n\n- `lead`: routes the issue.\n- `engineer`: writes the implementation plan.\n- `product-manager`, `architect`, `designer`, `documentation-planner`: optional roles the lead may request.\n\n## Config overrides\n\nSet these under `overrides` for `triage/<name>` (see [Configuration](../configuration.md)): `after` (roles whose plan sections must exist first), `plan_section` (the `plan.md` heading the role owns), `model`, `timeout`, `sidecar`. `intervals.triage` sets the poll interval and `require_approval` the approval gate.\nEOF\ncat > roles/workers.md <<'EOF'\n# Workers\n\n## What it does\n\nA worker implements the plan. It edits code, tests and docs in its own worktree on a `worker/...` branch. The controller then commits the change, runs the tests and opens a draft pull request. When a test run is red the worker gets fix rounds, and when a reviewer asks for changes it gets review rounds.\n\n## Inputs and outputs\n\n- **Inputs:** the issue, its comments, `plan.md`, and on a fix round `test-output.log`.\n- **Outputs:** code changes in the worktree and a report, `.ard/<issue#>/worker-<role>.md`.\n- A worker may never change `.github/`, `.ard/` (other than its report), secret-shaped files, symlinks, binaries or files over 1 MiB, nor more changed lines than `diff_max_lines`. Any other change ends the job `needs-human`.\n\n## Labels\n\n- `ard:worker:<role>` requests the worker once the plan is complete and approved.\n- `ard:review:<role>` is set on the pull request at close-out to request each review.\n- `ard:tests:failed` means the worker could not get the tests green.\n- `ard:needs-human` stops automation when the worker is blocked or the change is held.\n\n## Starter roles\n\n`engineer`.\n\n## Config overrides\n\nSet these under `overrides` for `workers/<name>` (see [Configuration](../configuration.md)): `tests`, `max_test_fixes`, `max_review_rounds`, `reviewers`, `diff_warn_lines`, `diff_max_lines`, `model`, `timeout`, `sidecar`.\nEOF\ncat > roles/reviewers.md <<'EOF'\n# Reviewers\n\n## What it does\n\nA reviewer critiques a draft pull request and gives a verdict. If it finds blocking issues, the pull request goes back to the worker for another review round. Once every requested review passes and the checks are green, the merger lands the pull request.\n\n## Inputs and outputs\n\n- **Inputs:** the pull request, its issue and `plan.md`.\n- **Outputs:** the role's own file, `.ard/<issue#>/<role>.md`, whose verdict becomes the review comment. Reviewers use their own verdicts rather than `done` and `blocked`.\n\n## Labels\n\n- `ard:review:<role>` requests a review on the pull request.\n- `ard:review:<role>:changes` sends the pull request back to the worker, up to `max_review_rounds` times.\n- `ard:needs-human` stops automation after the last round.\n- `ard:needs-approval` is set by the merger when the pull request touches a protected path.\n\n## Starter roles\n\n`eng-review`, `security-review`, `license-review`, `product-manager-review`.\n\n## Config overrides\n\nSet these under `overrides` for `reviewers/<name>` (see [Configuration](../configuration.md)): `model`, `timeout`, `sidecar`. Which reviewers run, and how many rounds, is set on the worker with `reviewers` and `max_review_rounds`. `intervals.reviewers` sets the poll interval.\nEOF\nsed -i 's#(roles.md)#(roles/index.md)#' security.md\ngit rm -q roles.md\ncd ../..\npython3 - <<'EOF'\np='mkdocs.yml'\ns=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace(\"  - search\\n\",\"  - search\\nmarkdown_extensions:\\n  - pymdownx.superfences:\\n      custom_fences:\\n        - name: mermaid\\n          class: mermaid\\n          format: !!python/name:pymdownx.superfences.fence_code_format\\n\")\ns=s.replace(\"  - Roles: roles.md\\n\",\"  - Roles:\\n      - Roles overview: roles/index.md\\n      - Scanners: roles/scanners.md\\n      - Triage: roles/triage.md\\n      - Workers: roles/workers.md\\n      - Reviewers: roles/reviewers.md\\n\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\np='docs/site/index.md'\ns=open(p).read()\nd='''```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    SC[\"Scanners<br/>file issues\"] --> I[\"Issue\"]\n    I -->|\"ard:pending\"| T[\"Triage<br/>lead, engineer, optional roles\"]\n    T --> P[\"plan.md\"]\n    P --> A{\"Human ard:approved<br/>(when require_approval)\"}\n    A --> W[\"Worker<br/>branch, tests, draft PR\"]\n    W --> R[\"Reviewers\"]\n    R -->|\"changes: review rounds,<br/>up to max_review_rounds\"| W\n    R -->|\"all approve: PR ready\"| M[\"Merger<br/>reviews at head, checks green;<br/>protected paths need a human\"]\n    M --> D[\"Merged, issue closed\"]\n    T -.-> H[\"ard:needs-human\"]\n    W -.-> H\n    R -.-> H\n    M -.-> H\n```\n\n'''\ns=s.replace(\"## The loop\\n\\n\",\"## The loop\\n\\n\"+d,1)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ngrep -rn 'roles\\.md' docs/site README.md mkdocs.yml\npython3 -m venv /tmp/mkv && /tmp/mkv/bin/pip -q install -r docs/site/requirements.txt 2>&1 | tail -2; PATH=/tmp/mkv/bin:$PATH make docs-check 2>&1 | tail -15"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1513,"cache_read_input_tokens":23585,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1513},"output_tokens":27,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"61c1f451-145c-4c06-8357-967729cffa65","uuid":"9da2a2c1-0947-4518-9d1a-4398a6cdf7fb","timestamp":"2026-11-25T12:58:52.776Z","request_id":"req_011ChPgjrDZASsRz11S6187Z"}
